ZM-49.994,11.451L-50.213,11.331L-50.063,10.841L-49.965,10.895L-49.936,11.062L-49.994,11.451ZM-50.51,10.855L-50.47,10.722L-50.313,10.68L-50.225,11.061L-50.608,11.093L-50.634,10.949L-50.51,10.855Z", "events": [{"year": -238, "title": "Mutineers driven out", "text": "After killing their Carthaginian officers, rebel mercenaries are expelled by the Sardinians and flee to Italy.", "lon": -52.363, "lat": -28.413}, {"year": -238, "title": "Rome's ultimatum", "text": "Answering the mutineers' appeal, Rome threatens war as Carthage prepares to retake the island.", "lon": -52.954, "lat": -30.324}, {"year": -238, "title": "Gracchus sails", "text": "The consul Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus leads the first Roman expedition to Sardinia.", "lon": -53.414, "lat": -31.541}, {"year": -238, "title": "Punic Tharros", "text": "Tharros, with its temples and harbour, has been a Carthaginian city for some three centuries.", "lon": -56.766, "lat": -22.765}, {"year": -237, "title": "Sardinia ceded", "text": "Exhausted by the Mercenary War, Carthage gives up Sardinia and agrees to pay Rome 1,200 talents more.", "lon": -56.701, "lat": -29.803}, {"year": -236, "title": "Corsican expedition", "text": "The consul Gaius Licinius Varus sends troops to Corsica; the Senate disowns his legate's treaty with the islanders.", "lon": -49.734, "lat": -3.476}, {"year": -236, "title": "Sardinians rise", "text": "Peoples of the plains take up arms against the new masters; Rome blames Carthaginian agents.", "lon": -54.072, "lat": -25.546}, {"year": -235, "title": "Torquatus triumphs", "text": "The consul Titus Manlius Torquatus subdues the rebels and celebrates a triumph over the Sardinians.", "lon": -54.729, "lat": -24.242}, {"year": -234, "title": "Carvilius campaigns", "text": "The consul Spurius Carvilius fights the Sardinians and later triumphs over them.", "lon": -53.743, "lat": -19.463}, {"year": -233, "title": "Pomponius triumphs", "text": "Manius Pomponius Matho celebrates another triumph over the Sardinians.", "lon": -55.715, "lat": -17.726}, {"year": -232, "title": "Both consuls sent", "text": "Both consuls of the year, Lepidus and Malleolus, campaign in Sardinia.", "lon": -55.978, "lat": -15.293}, {"year": -231, "title": "Hunted with dogs", "text": "Marcus Pomponius Matho brings hunting dogs from Italy to track Sardinians hiding in caves and forests.", "lon": -51.443, "lat": -20.767}, {"year": -231, "title": "The myrtle triumph", "text": "Gaius Papirius Maso subdues the Corsicans; refused a triumph, he holds his own on the Alban Mount, crowned with myrtle.", "lon": -52.429, "lat": -2.086}, {"year": -227, "title": "First praetor", "text": "Rome adds two praetors; one now governs Sardinia and Corsica every year.", "lon": -51.903, "lat": -28.065}, {"year": -225, "title": "Legion to Telamon", "text": "The consul Gaius Atilius Regulus ships his army from Sardinia to Pisa to meet an invading army of Gauls.", "lon": -47.171, "lat": -12.078}, {"year": -218, "title": "Hannibal's war", "text": "Hannibal marches on Italy; Sardinia becomes a front line of the war at sea.", "lon": -52.757, "lat": -27.718}, {"year": -217, "title": "Punic fleet off Sardinia", "text": "Seventy Carthaginian warships sail to Sardinia and on to Pisa, hoping to meet Hannibal.", "lon": -59.001, "lat": -16.422}, {"year": -217, "title": "Servilius' cruise", "text": "The consul Gnaeus Servilius Geminus sails round Sardinia and Corsica with 120 ships, taking hostages.", "lon": -48.485, "lat": -9.471}, {"year": -216, "title": "No pay, no grain", "text": "After Cannae, the praetor Aulus Cornelius Mammula reports that his troops lack pay and grain; Rome cannot help.", "lon": -52.166, "lat": -28.76}, {"year": -215, "title": "Envoys to Carthage", "text": "Sardinian leaders, led by the landowner Hampsicora of Cornus, secretly ask Carthage to retake the island.", "lon": -56.569, "lat": -21.114}, {"year": -215, "title": "Hasdrubal the Bald", "text": "Carthage sends Hasdrubal, nicknamed the Bald, with an army about as large as the one Mago had raised for Hannibal.", "lon": -54.4, "lat": -32.931}, {"year": -215, "title": "Blown off course", "text": "A storm drives Hasdrubal's fleet to the Balearic Islands, where his ships are hauled ashore for repairs.", "lon": -64.588, "lat": -30.759}, {"year": -215, "title": "The praetor falls ill", "text": "The praetor Quintus Mucius Scaevola, struck down by the island's fevers, cannot take the field.", "lon": -51.771, "lat": -28.239}, {"year": -215, "title": "Torquatus returns", "text": "Titus Manlius Torquatus, Sardinia's conqueror twenty years earlier, lands with a legion at Carales.", "lon": -52.691, "lat": -29.021}, {"year": -215, "title": "Sailors made soldiers", "text": "Torquatus arms his rowers and marines: 22,000 infantry and 1,200 cavalry.", "lon": -53.086, "lat": -27.892}, {"year": -215, "title": "Hampsicora recruits", "text": "Hampsicora goes into the mountains to raise the skin-clad highlanders, the Sardi Pelliti.", "lon": -51.771, "lat": -21.201}, {"year": -215, "title": "Hostus defeated", "text": "Left in command, Hampsicora's young son Hostus offers battle and is routed: 3,000 killed, 800 captured.", "lon": -54.4, "lat": -25.546}, {"year": -215, "title": "Flight to Cornus", "text": "The survivors of Hostus' army take refuge in Cornus, the rebels' chief town.", "lon": -56.241, "lat": -21.462}, {"year": -215, "title": "Hasdrubal lands", "text": "At last Hasdrubal's army lands, sends its ships home and joins Hampsicora.", "lon": -56.372, "lat": -22.07}, {"year": -215, "title": "March on Carales", "text": "The Punic and Sardinian army ravages the lands of Rome's allies on its way south.", "lon": -53.743, "lat": -26.675}, {"year": -215, "title": "Battle of Carales", "text": "After four hours of fighting the rebels break: about 12,000 dead and 3,700 taken prisoner.", "lon": -52.757, "lat": -27.196}, {"year": -215, "title": "Hostus killed", "text": "Hampsicora's son falls on the field as the Roman wing closes round the fleeing army.", "lon": -53.217, "lat": -26.849}, {"year": -215, "title": "Generals captured", "text": "Hasdrubal, the rebel leader Hanno and Mago, a kinsman of Hannibal, are taken prisoner.", "lon": -52.429, "lat": -26.849}, {"year": -215, "title": "Hampsicora's death", "text": "Fleeing with a few horsemen, Hampsicora learns of his son's death and kills himself in the night.", "lon": -54.729, "lat": -23.808}, {"year": -215, "title": "Cornus falls", "text": "Torquatus storms Cornus within days; the other rebel towns give hostages and pay tribute.", "lon": -56.766, "lat": -20.767}, {"year": -215, "title": "Seven ships taken", "text": "Off Sardinia, Titus Otacilius' fleet catches the Carthaginian ships heading home and captures seven.", "lon": -50.457, "lat": -32.757}, {"year": -215, "title": "Sardinia subdued", "text": "Torquatus sails back to Rome and reports the island fully subdued; it never returns to Carthage.", "lon": -52.231, "lat": -29.282}], "eras": [[-238, -237, "Rome Takes Sardinia"], [-236, -231, "The Sardinian Wars"], [-230, -219, "An Uneasy Province"], [-218, -216, "Hannibal's War"], [-215, -215, "Hampsicora's Revolt"]], "refLine": null, "metrics": [{"label": "Roman-held", "stages": [2]}, {"label": "Free highlands", "stages": [0]}], "chartNote": "Share of the area of Sardinia, by who held most of each of the island's eight former provinces (today's boundaries, not ancient ones). A simplification: the sources rarely say exactly where each revolt was fought, so the dates and extent of the uprisings of 236–231 BC are approximate, and Rome's hold on the hills was always thinner than the colours suggest. Corsica, ceded with Sardinia, is not coloured.", "chartTitle": "Sardinia by who held it, by area", "shares": [[0.5604, 0.4396, 0.0, 0.0], [0.5604, 0.0, 0.4396, 0.0], [0.5604, 0.0, 0.2493, 0.1902], [0.5604, 0.0, 0.2493, 0.1902], [0.5604, 0.0, 0.4396, 0.0], [0.3813, 0.0, 0.4396, 0.1791], [0.3813, 0.0, 0.4396, 0.1791], [0.3813, 0.0, 0.6187, 0.0], [0.3813, 0.0, 0.6187, 